WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.370
Who died and put Lucienne in charge?

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:05.109
And what are Desire's, well...desires?

00:00:05.109 --> 00:00:08.500
Here's what we know about the ending of "The
Sandman" Season 1.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.450
The final episode of "The Sandman" Season
1 shows the demon Azazel egging Lucifer Morningstar

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:19.060
on, with the hellish ruler plotting to use
the full might of their armies to bring Morpheus

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:20.189
to his knees.

00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:24.269
While this strongly hints at a full-blown
war between the demons of Hell and the forces

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:28.820
of the Dreaming, if the show follows the course
of the source material, this subplot will

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:33.950
likely end in Lucifer's retirement from being
the monarch of Hell, perhaps even following

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:38.880
a similar path to that of the Lucifer that
Tom Ellis portrayed in the series of the same name.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:44.240
Then again, the showrunners may subvert the
expectations of longtime fans, and things

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.530
could unfold on the TV show in an entirely
different manner.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:53.020
In an interview with TV Line, Gwendoline Christie
mused that the idea of losing to Dream must

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:58.030
be "entirely consuming" for the former favored
angel of God, perhaps enough for it to dwell

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:01.510
on their mind and dictate their actions in
future seasons.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:06.060
The loss in Season 1 could even be considered
the "final insult" for them.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.179
"Well, Lightbringer, it's your move."

00:01:10.179 --> 00:01:15.649
Additionally, Christie revealed that her portrayal
of Lucifer wasn't even inspired by Tom Ellis'

00:01:15.649 --> 00:01:19.170
version to begin with, despite being a fan
of the latter.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:23.900
She even avoided watching Ellis' show so that
it wouldn't influence her take on the role,

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.759
and instead opted to take inspiration from
assorted material.

00:01:28.259 --> 00:01:32.280
Introduced in Episode 1, the Corinthian serves
as a primary antagonist for the first season

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.090
of Netflix's "The Sandman."

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:39.039
The Corinthian's apparent obsession with killing
people in the Waking World, and the unmistakable

00:01:39.039 --> 00:01:44.259
joy he derives from taking a life, makes him
the ideal season-long foil for Morpheus.

00:01:44.259 --> 00:01:49.210
Aside from being an incredibly dangerous opponent,
the Corinthian also represents everything

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:51.750
that stands against Dream's principles.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:56.639
While Morpheus is all about maintaining balance,
adhering to predetermined roles, and restoring

00:01:56.639 --> 00:02:01.539
things to the way they were before his disappearance,
the Corinthian delights in becoming an agent

00:02:01.539 --> 00:02:03.429
of chaos and murder on Earth.

00:02:03.429 --> 00:02:07.529
He refuses to stick to the responsibilities
he was created to fulfill.

00:02:07.529 --> 00:02:12.400
In "The Sandman Companion" Neil Gaiman confirmed
that the nightmare's name came from the word

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.200
pertaining to the behavior typically attributed
to someone from the ancient Grecian region

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.540
of Corinth: reckless, lacking in morals, and
possessing a distaste for rules.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:26.400
Given how closely the series sticks to the
events of the source material, it's highly

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.798
likely that we'll see the Corinthian again
in a future season, though perhaps not anytime soon.

00:02:31.798 --> 00:02:36.370
Despite Dream's proclamation in Episode 10
that the world doesn't need another mouth-eyed

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:41.120
nightmare just yet, the comic book version
of the Lord of Dreams does end up remaking

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.010
a more loyal, less petty version of the Corinthian.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:49.440
The beginning of Netflix's "Sandman" series
shows how Dream was captured by Roderick Burgess

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.439
in 1916 and kept as a prisoner in his mansion
in Wych Cross, England.

00:02:54.439 --> 00:02:59.751
The ruler of the Dreaming is shown to be unwavering,
ruthless, and inflexible in his purpose as

00:02:59.751 --> 00:03:01.000
well as his resolve.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:06.099
He spends a century in his prison, remaining
completely silent while being guarded round

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:07.769
the clock by Burgess' men.

00:03:07.769 --> 00:03:12.440
And when an opportunity to escape arises,
Morpheus swiftly takes it.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.840
However, his quest to bring things back to
the way they were leads him to become gradually

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.390
but profoundly changed.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:22.830
He begins to realize that he has committed
mistakes during his rule as Dreamlord, and

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.439
also learns to be less rigid and more merciful.

00:03:25.439 --> 00:03:27.792
"It seems I owe you an apology.

00:03:27.792 --> 00:03:32.239
I've always heard it impolite to keep one's
friends waiting."

00:03:32.239 --> 00:03:36.909
He recognizes the efforts of his librarian
Lucienne to keep the Dreaming running in his

00:03:36.909 --> 00:03:37.909
extended absence.

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:42.019
He also accepts the harsh truth that he does
need friends, and even shows compassion in

00:03:42.019 --> 00:03:45.890
situations where he would normally make cold
calculated decisions.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:50.030
In an interview with TheWrap, Tom Sturridge
revealed that he followed two principles in

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:54.530
his portrayal of Morpheus: that his words
are calculated and precise, and that he is

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:57.620
terrifying and authoritative, yet seductive.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:01.640
This makes the character journey of someone
as set in his ways as Dream so fascinating

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.349
to watch throughout "The Sandman."

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:05.497
It's an evolution we're sure to see more of.

00:04:06.302 --> 00:04:09.930
For the uninitiated, one of the most shocking
things to come out of the first season of

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:14.720
"The Sandman" is the dream pregnancy of Lyta
Hall, which was somehow carried over to the

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.900
real world thanks to Rose Walker's powers
as a Dream Vortex.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:23.557
Lyta's child was conceived when she and her
dead husband Hector met and made love in Lyta's dream.

00:04:23.557 --> 00:04:29.598
In the "Sandman" Season 1 finale, Lyta gives
birth to a healthy child, who has yet to receive a name.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:34.990
After surviving her ordeal with Morpheus thanks
to her grandmother Unity Kincaid's ultimate

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:39.810
sacrifice, Rose commits to raising the child
alongside her brother Jed and Lyta.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:43.860
What new fans of "The Sandman" may not realize,
though, is that the series may have actually

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:48.100
planted the seeds of its finale as early as
this sequence of events.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:53.510
In the comics, the child, who is the son of
established DC superheroes Dr. Fate and Fury,

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.480
receives the name Daniel from Morpheus himself.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:00.970
Through a series of complicated and ultimately
tragic events, Daniel grows up and discovers

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:03.840
that he has the power to cross over to the
Dreaming.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.810
Eventually, he becomes the new ruler of the
Dreaming when Morpheus dies at the hands of

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:10.889
the Kindly Ones, becoming the new "Dream of
the Endless."

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:16.169
Despite the Corinthian's attempts to trick
Rose Walker into using her Dream Vortex abilities

00:05:16.169 --> 00:05:21.139
to destroy Morpheus, the young woman decides
to sacrifice herself to ensure the survival

00:05:21.139 --> 00:05:22.410
of her loved ones.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:27.220
She avoids this fate, however, thanks to the
intervention of two characters: the enigmatic

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:31.860
Gilbert, who is secretly an escapee from the
Dreaming called Fiddler's Green, and Rose's

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:37.009
grandmother Unity Kincaid, who takes the Dream
Vortex from Rose so that she could die in

00:05:37.009 --> 00:05:39.120
her great granddaughter's place.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:45.000
In an interview with Popverse conducted during
SDCC 2022, Vanesu Samunyai shared that in

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:49.240
bringing Rose the Dream Vortex to life, she
had to work on building the character's back

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:50.720
story on her own.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:54.460
This was because they were using an early
storyline from the comics that covered only

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:56.259
a fraction of Rose's development.

00:05:56.259 --> 00:06:00.710
She also described her role in a way that
hinted that there's more to come for the character

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:01.840
in future seasons.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.400
Plus, given Rose's commitment at the end of
the series to take care of her brother Jed

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.790
and Lyta's currently unnamed child, it's almost
a certainty that we'll see the character again,

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:14.349
maybe even as early as Season 2.

00:06:14.349 --> 00:06:19.428
Perhaps her time as a Dream Vortex will come
in handy when she guides Lyta's child into adulthood.

00:06:19.867 --> 00:06:25.389
In Dream's absence, the librarian Lucienne
takes it upon herself to maintain order in the Dreaming.

00:06:25.389 --> 00:06:29.360
Whereas the other enchanted denizens of the
realm lost faith in Morpheus and left the

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:34.069
kingdom, Lucienne stayed, firmly believing
that Dream would return someday.

00:06:34.069 --> 00:06:38.720
When Morpheus finally does make his return,
he starts by being largely dismissive and

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.880
unappreciative of Lucienne's thoughts and
opinions.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:46.490
By season's end, though, Dream places the
day-to-day operations of the realm in Lucienne's

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:52.030
capable hands once again, as he focuses on
creating a new batch of dreams and nightmares.

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:58.069
"Would you mind taking care of things while
I work?"

00:06:58.069 --> 00:07:02.131
From the many interactions between Morpheus
and Lucienne throughout "The Sandman," it's

00:07:02.131 --> 00:07:06.640
clear that the Dreaming's librarian has a
deep respect and reverence for Dream.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:10.699
Despite Dream's apparent disregard for her
efforts, Lucienne continues to work behind

00:07:10.699 --> 00:07:13.520
the scenes to prevent the kingdom from collapsing.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:17.440
This is partly because she's aware of the
tremendous weight on Dream's shoulders and

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:18.940
wants to save him from self-ruin.

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:24.560
Vivienne Acheampong, affirmed this in an interview
with Collider, calling the relationship between

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:28.090
the lord and the librarian "very important
and special."

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:32.785
It's almost guaranteed at this point that
we'll see Lucienne in future seasons of "The Sandman."

00:07:32.785 --> 00:07:37.669
Unless the series decides to deviate radically
from the source material, the librarian of

00:07:37.669 --> 00:07:41.768
the Dreaming will likely continue to be Dream's
most trusted servant.

00:07:41.768 --> 00:07:47.770
In Episode 10 of "The Sandman" Season 1, we
see Dream confront his sibling Desire in the latter's realm.

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:52.800
As it turns out, the misfortunes that befell
Dream in the Waking World were brought about by Desire.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:57.419
In their conversation with the fellow Endless
known as Despair, it becomes clear that Desire

00:07:57.419 --> 00:08:01.990
harbors massive enmity towards Dream, and
is willing to do anything to bring the master

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:03.689
of the Dreaming down a few notches.

00:08:03.689 --> 00:08:08.409
While "The Sandman" Season 1 doesn't explore
the deeper reasons behind Desire's malicious

00:08:08.409 --> 00:08:12.860
grudge against Dream, their heated exchange
during the last episode promises that we'll

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:16.810
see more of this sibling rivalry in subsequent
seasons.

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:21.490
Interestingly, despite the disastrous consequences
of Desire's actions, Dream's sibling doesn't

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:25.120
see themself as a villain, but as the hero
of their own story.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:30.360
Mason Alexander Park, who plays Desire, states
this in an interview published by FanSided,

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:34.400
adding that the character only really comes
into conflict with their older brother because

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:37.169
of their disparity in morals and character
traits.

00:08:37.169 --> 00:08:41.459
Among the seven siblings who comprise the
Endless, audiences have only been formally

00:08:41.459 --> 00:08:45.779
introduced to Dream, Desire, Despair, and
Death.

00:08:45.779 --> 00:08:48.940
Their "prodigal" brother Destruction was referred
to, but not name-dropped.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:51.760
"The Prodigal has returned?"

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:53.040
"What?

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:56.880
Oh, him, no."

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:00.800
We don't get to see Delirium and Destiny in
Season 1, but they're bound to show up in

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:05.320
future seasons, especially since Park expressed
their excitement at being part of a scene

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:07.364
where the siblings come together.

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:12.579
Longtime fans of "The Sandman" may not recognize
Gault, who plays a fairly significant role

00:09:12.579 --> 00:09:13.589
in the first season.

00:09:13.589 --> 00:09:17.990
That's because she's a character created for
the series, combining two nightmares from

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:22.339
the comics (Brute and Glob) while retaining
the same narrative role they filled.

00:09:22.339 --> 00:09:26.610
Much like the Corinthian, Gault serves as
an effective obstacle in Dream's way because

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:29.610
she does not want to follow her predetermined
path.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:33.740
Her desire to become a vision that inspires
hope, rather than one that brings a person's

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:38.370
fear to the surface, which is her actual role
as a nightmare, is what prompted her to escape

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:40.310
from the Dreaming in the first place.

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:44.930
To watch this contrast play out in the few
episodes she's in is a treat, especially because

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:47.880
it doesn't get immediately resolved when she
confronts Dream.

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:52.860
Rather, we see her get her wish in Episode
10 when Morpheus remakes her into a dream,

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:54.440
complete with beautiful wings.

00:09:54.440 --> 00:10:00.000
This represents a significant change not only
in Gault, but in the formerly inflexible Dream as well.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:02.339
In a way, her rebellion helped both of them
to grow.

00:10:02.339 --> 00:10:06.980
"I had no right returning here after over
a century expecting everything to be just

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:08.000
as I left it."

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:12.061
Since Gault's character journey has already
come full circle by the end of Season 1, it's

00:10:12.061 --> 00:10:15.120
unlikely that we'll see her pop up in future
seasons.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:19.180
Never say never, though, as her newfound role
and abilities may still play a part in Dream's

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:20.180
future adventures.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:24.850
Fans who read the source material may have
expected to see some familiar faces from the

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:28.210
DC Universe in Season 1 of Netflix's "The
Sandman."

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:33.180
For starters, two superheroes strongly affiliated
with the Justice League: Mister Miracle in

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:37.790
his civilian identity, as well as the Martian
Manhunter, appear during the first arc of

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:39.120
the comic book series.

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:43.930
The demon Etrigan served as Dream's guide
when he ventured to Hell to retrieve his helmet.

00:10:43.930 --> 00:10:48.810
Perhaps most significantly, Morpheus originally
had to fight the DC supervillain Doctor Destiny

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:51.930
to regain the powers contained in his ruby.

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:56.459
The Netflix series does utilize John Dee in
this specific role, but never refers to him

00:10:56.459 --> 00:10:58.540
as his nefarious alter-ego.

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:02.890
While viewers cannot be faulted for speculating
that the reason for their omission was strictly

00:11:02.890 --> 00:11:07.500
due to various licensing issues, the reality
appears to be a lot less corporate-driven

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:11.060
than that, at least according to "Sandman"
creator Neil Gaiman.

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:17.120
In a 2021 Tumblr post, a fan asked Gaiman
about his decision to incorporate established

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:19.560
DC characters into "The Sandman."

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:24.260
The author responded that while it was his
desire to tap into "the fabric of the DC universe"

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:27.029
while writing "Sandman," the book, for the
show,

00:11:27.029 --> 00:11:31.459
"[It] seemed naturally to drift away from
the DC Universe into [a universe] that looked

00:11:31.459 --> 00:11:32.499
a lot more like ours."

